swer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c405fee2-0e70-5609-a50b-a2f6fe31bc07" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8u + 6v &=3 \\\\ -4u + 5v &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $-4$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&8u + 6v = 3 \\\\ &(-4u + 5v = 8)\\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-8u + 10v &= 16 \\\\\n(6v + 10v) &= (3 + 16) \\\\\n16v &= 19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93dcc73c-ce2d-5fa3-b32b-9fc600fb269e" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65d8927d-3570-5e0f-a41d-d32ff6b3dcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509173} {:answer/id #uuid "70f3e0b1-c6be-57d7