en we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-3u + 15v &= 24 \\\\\n(6v + 15v) &= (3 + 24) \\\\\n21v &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e9c308-67f3-5e17-a945-9f7ca8b964e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e9c308-67f3-5e17-a945-9f7ca8b964e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509311} :answer-field/id #uuid "147b96a0-420d-536f-930a-bf1ab6057f76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fee1e55-5f1d-5256-8a99-8662fe215f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fee1e55-5f1d-5256-8a99-8662fe215f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509268} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7e7709b-4144-5bdd-89e3-266ed5cdd337" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15551398-1d65-5cfc-9388-3441da458ef3" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+y&=16\\\\-7x-6y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&\\left(5x+y=16\\right)\\cdot (6) \\\\ &-7x-6y=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30x+6y&=96 \\\\\n-7x-6y&=-4 \\\\\n(30x-7x)&=(96-4) \\\\\n23x&=92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+y&=16 \\\\\n5(4)+y&=16 \\\\\n20+y&=16 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200edcc6-55df-50a4-aa4a-486132c110af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{28}{11},- \\frac{339}{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509269} {:answer/id #uuid "59eca543-875a-5f9c-906c-2227556ef87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509292} {:answer/id #uuid "9bae6aad-69de-515b-ae98-f68c8a8cd5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{15}{11},- \\frac{124}{11})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509315} {:answer/id #uuid "c8204af7-b37e-5cf3-8cb6-a7dd64791ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509329} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2229f3-7217-5ef8-ada8-8b297feb7340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8204af7-b37e-5cf3-8cb6-a7dd64791ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509329} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87fcfbe-ec51-5267-986b-e924e05d49d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21904fad-7e59-5888-9a6b-ef099e3648bc" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+6y&=-17\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&x+6y=-17 \\\\ &\\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-3)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+6y&=-17 \\\\\n21x-6y&=39 \\\\\n(x+21x)&=(-17+39) \\\\\n22x&=22 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f908001-8c0e-5ad5-bf2c-a4aae8e1a691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f908001-8c0e-5ad5-bf2c-a4aae8e1a691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509264} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc24fa13-ffeb-5ff7-9dbb-b1d479e2964b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e86f70f-2c51-5c48-9d22-0d5dc6889c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e86f70f-2c51-5c48-9d22-0d5dc6889c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509263} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5fd3803-15e2-50c5-94bb-4bdef29b8a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23b70eba-8c0c-5171-b139-0ac2370d20a6" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+14y&=-36\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $14$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&5x+14y=-36 \\\\ &\\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (7)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+14y&=-36 \\\\\n-49x-14y&=-140 \\\\\n(5x-49x)&=(-36-140) \\\\\n-44x&=-176 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b382fd6-c1bc-58a5-ab3b-203bfcd1d3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509275} {:answer/id #uuid "35155a6e-f543-56cc-895d-88462084d35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509277} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee68739-5943-5dba-a7b3-6a408d7f228a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509309} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7dc056-edde-5f96-a671-5ccf85ff5755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{64}{5},- \\frac{99}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509324} {:answer/id #uuid "f8dcd307-0ccd-537f-902c-16a48c2e11dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35155a6e-f543-56cc-895d-88462084d35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509277} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0f030-a9e9-5940-9a6d-793769aabcc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3775b5ec-0d42-5ee6-ba36-8700f462d97a" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y&=-7\\\\x-6y&=22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&\\left(-x+y=-7\\right)\\cdot (6) \\\\ &x-6y=22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x+6y&=-42 \\\\\nx-6y&=22 \\\\\n(-6x+x)&=(-42+22) \\\\\n-5x&=-20 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+y&=-7 \\\\\n-1(4)+y&=-7 \\\\\n-4+y&=-7 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cfe277-3842-5747-9ee0-6c4247fead55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509273} {:answer/id #uuid "450b54a2-ea5c-555a-b4d6-fe9b7a919372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509283} {:answer/id #uuid "97dffbe1-1bb5-5124-8577-4fb6de014f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{23}{13},\\frac{264}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509313} {:answer/id #uuid "a2934f92-f656-5bd6-8747-1b6db11bc275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509316} {:answer/id #uuid "f434a981-e2a2-536d-8559-c0efa231bcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{68}{13},\\frac{399}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26cfe277-3842-5747-9ee0-6c4247fead55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509273} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b38b033-45ad-5b3e-b165-1a07d9c9cdc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575d3d25-ee48-560a-beb0-60eb637f5795" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y&=8\\\\3x+y&=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&-x+4y=8 \\\\ &\\left(3x+y=15\\right)\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+4y&=8 \\\\\n-12x-4y&=-60 \\\\\n(-x-12x)&=(8-60) \\\\\n-13x&=-52 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+y&=15 \\\\\n3(4)+y&=15 \\\\\n12+y&=15 \\\\\ny&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = 3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e27713-0c32-5160-bf48-8794b4ef56ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509265} {:answer/id #uuid "37d9b91c-a905-57ce-81a0-85f4eccb157f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509279} {:answer/id #uuid "82de173e-ffef-54ef-bc13-55db11bad3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,- \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fbed6e-fb34-5ad3-8ef9-483b59c4fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509325} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a705a0-e0c3-5f06-aaae-681a63958822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1fbed6e-fb34-5ad3-8ef9-483b59c4fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509325} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e82569-1864-5896-a52b-68af45977480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78207f23-12ee-53c5-9f78-9395f0921474" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y&=5\\\\7x-6y&=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&\\left(x-2y=5\\right)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &7x-6y=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x+6y&=-15 \\\\\n7x-6y&=11 \\\\\n(-3x+7x)&=(-15+11) \\\\\n4x&=-4 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx-2y&=5 \\\\\n1(-1)-2y&=5 \\\\\n-1-2y&=5 \\\\\n-2y&=6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dff6f0-44a2-569f-9cf5-16833973d9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{48}{43},- \\frac{895}{86})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509276} {:answer/id #uuid "5445dbb7-2643-5c0f-a09e-cd8ccf6fd047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509286} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba4ae51-6c73-5240-afd2-12a95ee94548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509297} {:answer/id #uuid "77ffeb13-0d3e-595c-929a-d62967cf28a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509301} {:answer/id #uuid "cd313717-399a-59fe-9882-55714ce7fdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{113}{43},- \\frac{675}{43})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77ffeb13-0d3e-595c-929a-d62967cf28a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509301} :answer-field/id #uuid "b210daf5-f7c9-544f-a57c-87c7b7a3a074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "888444ab-20f1-5ac7-85e7-9089399fd1bb" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+12y&=-35\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $12$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $12$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&x+12y=-35 \\\\ &\\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-6)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+12y&=-35 \\\\\n42x-12y&=78 \\\\\n(x+42x)&=(-35+78) \\\\\n43x&=43 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c580255-e760-5f33-8c6a-2550a573a799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-22,52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509262} {:answer/id #uuid "57ef6484-bebb-5270-9134-2a94851e1343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509289} {:answer/id #uuid "65e68218-6593-5454-a9fc-d17eedb52630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{10}{11},- \\frac{8}{11})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509295} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0f169-c30c-552b-b0d7-efdfc48e9c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509335} {:answer/id #uuid "eadf7382-2d55-5dc8-a450-064d2a4cf6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ef6484-bebb-5270-9134-2a94851e1343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509289} :answer-field/id #uuid "2658620e-2fcd-55d0-9ddc-b610c83e058d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90d0118c-789c-54fc-9fd4-a48e58b92b30" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-2y&=6\\\\3x+10y&=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $10$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-10$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&\\left(-5x-2y=6\\right)\\cdot (5) \\\\ &3x+10y=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-25x-10y&=30 \\\\\n3x+10y&=14 \\\\\n(-25x+3x)&=(30+14) \\\\\n-22x&=44 \\\\\nx&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -2$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x-2y&=6 \\\\\n-5(-2)-2y&=6 \\\\\n10-2y&=6 \\\\\n-2y&=-4 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -2, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-2,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d2421c4-3e95-5a88-8c94-267fc39c0f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509298} {:answer/id #uuid "7135885d-50ad-52a2-b1d8-046d4c8b8464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509299} {:answer/id #uuid "7935b5f6-9317-5d13-a182-f177d4cf51e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509303} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac1beda-ed4c-5a56-9a2c-7d43cc5905f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509304} {:answer/id #uuid "b920b780-bd99-5c37-9e3d-f93a43c3c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ac1beda-ed4c-5a56-9a2c-7d43cc5905f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a96850-9a06-5e2c-8ce5-899e594c0915" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad8b1c30-588c-535a-9132-16c79af9dfe2" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+4y&=-1\\\\x+2y&=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&5x+4y=-1 \\\\ &\\left(x+2y=1\\right)\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+4y&=-1 \\\\\n-2x-4y&=-2 \\\\\n(5x-2x)&=(-1-2) \\\\\n3x&=-3 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+2y&=1 \\\\\n1(-1)+2y&=1 \\\\\n-1+2y&=1 \\\\\n2y&=2 \\\\\ny&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = 1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66300ce6-b475-5d0f-b089-ac8eec2807a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66300ce6-b475-5d0f-b089-ac8eec2807a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509296} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fec54cb-9c85-5252-8582-cea05545c3ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5709927f-d51f-5749-a92a-072b5998a2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5709927f-d51f-5749-a92a-072b5998a2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509287} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3de5a92-faa1-5773-9a97-9f5b9fa3c657" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4b00c48-3459-54dd-a97b-4752b54d2e62" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y&=-12\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&5x+8y=-12 \\\\ &\\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+8y&=-12 \\\\\n-28x-8y&=-80 \\\\\n(5x-28x)&=(-12-80) \\\\\n-23x&=-92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4363e1-914f-5143-818c-f9a250cf4675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{19},- \\frac{265}{57})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509280} {:answer/id #uuid "58d587da-c716-5846-9cf4-4ad93c3d7829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509291} {:answer/id #uuid "93e6532a-ea86-5e9b-b388-cca7bcfcdbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509312} {:answer/id #uuid "b17056e7-740c-572d-a1a8-6081d649e85d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{95}{3},\\frac{151}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509319} {:answer/id #uuid "d05f6b87-d487-5741-a054-888fc5a7888a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,- \\frac{23}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e6532a-ea86-5e9b-b388-cca7bcfcdbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509312} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e1e4bf-ace0-570f-9bd6-71262f095a75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5c3f388-7843-5364-910c-9e97bb540c6d" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y&=13\\\\5x-21y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-3$, while in the second equation it is $-21$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $21$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-21$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&\\left(-2x-3y=13\\right)\\cdot (-7) \\\\ &5x-21y=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14x+21y&=-91 \\\\\n5x-21y&=-4 \\\\\n(14x+5x)&=(-91-4) \\\\\n19x&=-95 \\\\\nx&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -5$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x-3y&=13 \\\\\n-2(-5)-3y&=13 \\\\\n10-3y&=13 \\\\\n-3y&=3 \\\\\ny&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -5, \\,y = -1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-5,-1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbbb5e-3ed2-528e-830c-64e987e989da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509281} {:answer/id #uuid "42e7ebb4-26b8-5fd9-b9bf-6edb7cf93ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509282} {:answer/id #uuid "843072f9-f614-5e55-8bf0-12fd95bdb012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509307} {:answer/id #uuid "991361f4-364a-57db-97a8-04a5f7037455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509314} {:answer/id #uuid "ad644440-f651-5dad-b438-815e63a116ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,- \\frac{29}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e7ebb4-26b8-5fd9-b9bf-6edb7cf93ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509282} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bccdb9c-e6cc-546c-bc1f-af909a73f4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef7f3552-0ad8-5545-8673-2b2894d40ad4" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+6y&=-33\\\\2x-3y&=21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&-x+6y=-33 \\\\ &\\left(2x-3y=21\\right)\\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+6y&=-33 \\\\\n4x-6y&=42 \\\\\n(-x+4x)&=(-33+42) \\\\\n3x&=9 \\\\\nx&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x-3y&=21 \\\\\n2(3)-3y&=21 \\\\\n6-3y&=21 \\\\\n-3y&=15 \\\\\ny&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 3, \\,y = -5\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,-5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d9b9f6-af1e-5d04-81f6-d3e14d15ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{33}{5},- \\frac{166}{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509274} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee1735-757a-5a7e-8abc-3d08ac98a01a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{19}{33},\\frac{562}{99})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509284} {:answer/id #uuid "581c1cfe-6f4b-5f91-ad28-ceabe0e592f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509290} {:answer/id #uuid "61a29bde-d4a9-528b-90d5-e13febae164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509294} {:answer/id #uuid "b681415e-0e9c-5a64-970d-72037254e2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{20}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581c1cfe-6f4b-5f91-ad28-ceabe0e592f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509290} :answer-field/id #uuid "8275be3d-f06e-52aa-9799-9ef432738ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe868340-7a35-5fed-a08d-ce25cbc1fbb9" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+15y&=32\\\\-7x-3y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $15$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $15$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&2x+15y=32 \\\\ &\\left(-7x-3y=-13\\right)\\cdot (5)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+15y&=32 \\\\\n-35x-15y&=-65 \\\\\n(2x-35x)&=(32-65) \\\\\n-33x&=-33 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-3y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ee3752b-c151-5d7c-b459-269e7a440470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529275} {:answer/id #uuid "ad343ca9-8ee8-59ee-8492-d87f4e5767a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529312} {:answer/id #uuid "afaa3c2f-68a9-5e46-b525-4af2bb7fbccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad343ca9-8ee8-59ee-8492-d87f4e5767a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529312} :answer-field/id #uuid "431a38a6-e58c-5853-ad1f-a6fc6bb01e65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17e3609b-1396-5685-843a-74d27fdadf0f" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $2x$ term that can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6 && \\mid \\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= 6 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n-3y &= (6 + 12) \\\\\n-3y &= 18 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + (1)(-6) &= 6 \\\\\n-2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(-6,-6)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a19f9f-c97f-5b22-a3fb-3ddd84649033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529285} {:answer/id #uuid "7968a19d-81e4-508f-8ec6-11e1b7db0381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529288} {:answer/id #uuid "cd558f16-0c95-5532-85ce-16a34dea6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7968a19d-81e4-508f-8ec6-11e1b7db0381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88aecce-0e48-579b-8ac8-fffb289b1a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22c76199-015b-5355-8da5-51b3e4eb6386" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y + 2x + 4 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 4x + 9 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y &=-4 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 4y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n6y &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n6y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (1)(- \\frac{17}{6}) &= -4 \\\\\n2x &= - \\frac{7}{6} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{7}{12},- \\frac{17}{6})$."}]}
